"0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="600"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Removed by detergents that disrupt the membrane</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="300"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Purified integral membrane proteins still have phospholipids associated with them.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:cxnSp><p:nvCxnSpPr><p:cNvPr id="306" name="Google Shape;306;p42"/><p:cNvCxnSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="457200" y="762000"/><a:ext cx="8229600" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/><a:tailEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="An image labeled at the top “integral proteins.” A cell membrane with multicolored hydrophilic heads interacts with multiple proteins. To the right end of the membrane there is a large membrane-spanning protein. It is yellow in the middle, among the tails, and is blue at either end. It is long, with a lobed top with 3 + signs. It is labeled “polytopic” on the left. Above this protein are 2 large blue proteins. One bonds to the lobed top of the integral protein; there is a Ca2+ on the right end (labeled). On the left, an upward curved arrow shows the release of the large blue protein; it appears detached above. Text to the left of the arrow reads, “change in pH; chelating agent; urea; carbonate.” At the bottom of the membrane is a protein labeled “monotopic”; it is smaller and does not span the membrane. Instead, it only reaches about halfway up to the top. The topmost portion, which is in embedded inside the membrane, is yellow, and the bottom portion outside the membrane is blue. Below this diagram is an illustration of a cluster of detergent molecules, shown as pink lipid molecules with heads and tails. From this come two curved arrows. The left arrow indicates the monotopic protein coated on its yellow end with pink lipid molecules; the right arrow indicates the polytopic protein coated in both sides of its yellow region (because it takes up the entire middle portion of the protein). " id="307" name="Google Shape;307;p42"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="4849812" y="990600"/><a:ext cx="3989387" cy="5576887"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>